      "type": "thinking",
      "thinking": "Let me analyze this OIML certificate carefully.\n\nThis is an OIML Certificate R 49-1 (2013) for water meters — AquaMaster4 electromagnetic water meters from ABB.\n\nKey document-level info:\n- Number: R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01 revision 12\n- Scheme: A\n- Project number: 3903947\n- Page total: 13\n- Member state: The Netherlands\n- Date issued: 7 April 2025\n- OIML issuer ID: NL1\n\nIssuing authority:\n- Name: NMi Certin B.V. (OIML Issuing Authority NL1)\n- Address: Thijsseweg 11, 2629 JA Delft, The Netherlands\n- Person responsible: M.Ph.D. Schmidt\n- Phone: +31 88 636 2332\n- Email: certin@nmi.nl\n- Website: www.nmi.nl\n- oiml_issuer_id: NL1\n\nApplicant and Manufacturer:\n- ABB Limited, Oldends Lane, GL10 3TA Stonehouse, United Kingdom\n\nAdditional production locations (page 13) — these are manufacturers/producers:\n- ABB Engineering (Shanghai) Ltd., No. 4528, Kangxin Highway, Pudong New District, Shanghai, 201319, P.R. China\n- ABB Inc., 125 E. County Line Road, Warminster, PA 18974, USA\n- ABB India Limited, Plot No. 5 & 6, 2nd Phase, Peenya Industrial Area, Bengaluru – 560058, Karnataka India\n\nThe certificate says \"Applicant and Manufacturer\" is ABB Limited. Then page 13 says the water meter is produced at one of the following production locations — ABB Limited plus three others. I'll include those as manufacturers too (production locations).\n\nCertified type:\n- Category: \"Water meter\"\n- Type designations: AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z and FET4* — actually \"Type: AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z and FET4 *\". Hmm, this is odd. The types are FEW4XY.Z (flow transmitter?) and FET4* (flow sensor?). Actually AquaMaster4 consists of flow sensor FET4xx and transmitter FEW4xx. The \"Type: AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z and FET4 *\". \n\nThe X/Y/Z breakdown:\n- X being 1 for standard, 3 for advanced, 5 for premium (mobile Comms - MC) variant\n- Y being 1, 2, 8 or 9 (integral/remote version, optionally sensor only)\n- Z being R (reduced bore), V (virtual full bore) or F (full bore)\n\nSo type designations: \"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z\", \"FET4*\". The tables mention transmitter types FEW41Y.Z, FEW43Y.Z, FEW45Y.Z.\n\nType designations list: [\"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z\", \"FET4\", \"FEW41Y.Z\", \"FEW43Y.Z\", \"FEW45Y.Z\"] — hmm, FEW41Y.Z etc. are variants within the family. I'd list \"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z\" and \"FET4*\" as the type designations, maybe also include the specific transmitter types.\n\nModule designation: null or \"Not applicable\"? The cert doesn't mention module. Omit or set null.\n\nDescription: \"An electromagnetic water meter\" — Type: AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z and FET4*.\n\nRecommendation: R 49-1 (2013) → id \"R49\", edition... The recommendation is \"R 49-1 (2013)\". Hmm, R49-1 is part 1 of R49. Edition 2013. Should id be \"R49\" or \"R49-1\"? The canonical form per example \"R60\" not \"OIML R 60\". I'll use \"R49-1\" since that's the specific part, edition 2013. Actually the schema says id canonical e.g. \"R60\". R49-1 is a part of R49. I'll use \"R49-1\" as the id to be precise, edition 2013. Hmm, edition is int per schema: edition (int). So edition: 2013. Accuracy classes: [\"1\", \"2\"].\n\nTest reports:\n- NMi-2254799-01 dated 14 August 2020, 235 pages\n- NMi-2254799-03 dated 30 September 2020, 44 pages\n- NMi-2254799-04 dated 24 December 2020, 180 pages\n- NMi-2564429-01 dated 2 August 2021, 26 pages\n- NMi-2496280-01 dated 26 August 2021, 29 pages\n- NMi-2496280-02 dated 23 November 2021, 25 pages\n- NMi-2563125-01 dated 22 September 2023, 94 pages\n\nRole: these are OIML Type Evaluation Reports. The cert says \"the associated reports\". The certificate mentions \"OIML Type Evaluation Report\". I'll set role: \"type_evaluation_report\" for all. Documentation folder no. T11851-9 → documentation_file.\n\nDocumentation file: \"The construction of the measuring instrument is recorded in the Documentation folder no. T11851-9.\" → test_reports[] entry: {id: \"T11851-9\", role: \"documentation_file\"}.\n\nRevision history: 13 entries (Initial + 1..12).\n- Initial, 14 August 2020, First issue.\n- 1, 30 September 2020, Additional evaluation of reduced bore larger sizes up to DN600.\n- 2, 24 December 2020, ...\n- ... etc through 12, 7 April 2025.\n\nRevision as string: \"initial\", \"1\"...\"12\".\n\nFootnotes: \n- [1] \"The software version and checksum are displayed on the meter during the circular sequence every 10 minutes. The software version and checksum are displayed sequentially with first for the Measurement part then the Communications part.\" This footnote is attached to Table 4 title. I'll include it.\n\nAlso the asterisk notes about X/Y/Z — those are type designation conventions, not footnotes per se. Could include as footnotes? They're marked with \"*\" after \"FET4 *\". I could add footnote marker \"*\" with text \"With X being 1 for standard or 3 for advanced or 5 for premium (mobile Comms - MC) variant; Y being 1, 2, 8 or 9 (integral/remote version, optionally sensor only); Z being R (reduced bore), V (virtual full bore) or F (full bore).\" That seems reasonable.\n\nCharacteristics:\n\nType level (from Tables 1 & 2 — common characteristics):\n- measuring_principle: \"Electromagnetic\"\n- accuracy_class: [\"1\",\"2\"] — this is a list; could put in recommendation.accuracy_classes and type_level as value \"1 and 2\"? I'll put accuracy_class in type_level as a string \"1 and 2\" or list. Structured values value can be string. I'll do value: \"1 and 2\". Hmm, or maybe omit since it's in recommendation. I'll include it as string.\n- environmental_class: \"O (installed outdoors)\"\n- electromagnetic_environment: \"E2\" (Tables 1); Table 2 says E1 for transmitter with external antenna cable, E2 without. This varies → config_level maybe. I'll record electromagnetic_environment at type_level \"E2\" and add config_level entry for FEW45 transmitter: condition \"transmitter with external antenna cable\" → \"E1\", \"transmitter without external antenna cable\" → \"E2\".\n- mechanical_class: \"M1\"\n- temperature_range_ambient: {min: -25, max: 55}, unit \"°C\"\n- water_temperature_class: \"T50\" with range +0.1 to +50 °C. Value: \"T50 (+0.1 °C / +50 °C)\"? Better: attribute water_temperature_class value \"T50\", and separate attribute? The instruction: extract English. I'd do water_temperature_class: {value: \"T50\"} and maybe water_temperature_range: {min: 0.1, max: 50, unit: °C}. I'll do both.\n- maximum_admissible_pressure: 1.6 MPa (also 16 bar). value 1.6, unit MPa. Could add second entry maximum_admissible_pressure_bar: 16 bar. I'll do MAP value 1.6 MPa and note alternative 16 bar as separate attribute \"maximum_admissible_pressure_alt\": 16 bar. Hmm, keep simple: one entry with value 1.6 unit MPa.\n- orientation: \"All positions (horizontal, vertical, or diagonal)\"\n- flow_profile_sensitivity_class: string \"U0 and D0 (0 x DN upstream and 0 x DN downstream); U3 and D0 for full bore DN250 and larger\" — this is config-dependent. Could be config_level with axis \"bore size\". I'll record as a string at type_level since it's text. Or config_level: attribute flow_profile_sensitivity_class, axis \"bore/type\", values: {condition: \"full bore DN250 and larger\", value: \"U3 and D0 (3 x DN upstream, 0 x DN downstream)\"}, {condition: \"default\", value: \"U0 and D0 (0 x DN upstream, 0 x DN downstream)\"}. That's nicer. Let me do config_level.\n- reverse_flow: \"The sensor is intended to measure reverse flow\" — string true-ish. value: \"intended to measure reverse flow\".\n- pressure_loss_class: varies by bore and size → config_level with axis \"bore / meter size\". Values:\n  - reduced bore, DN40–DN50: Δp 40 (0.40 bar)\n  - reduced bore, >DN50: Δp 63 (0.63 bar)\n  - full bore, DN40–DN50: Δp 10 (0.10 bar)\n  - full bore, DN65–DN200: Δp 16 (0.16 bar)\n  - full bore, ≥DN250: Δp 10 (0.10 bar)\n- power_supply: varies by transmitter type (FEW41/43 vs FEW45) and mode:\n  - Table 1 (FEW41/43): AC mains 95–240 VAC @ 50/60 Hz; renewable/DC 6–32 V; replaceable battery 2x 3.6 V (op range 2.9–3.6 V)\n  - Table 2 (FEW45): AC mains 95–240 VAC @ 50/60 Hz incl 4x 3.6 V batteries; renewable 6–32 V incl 4x 3.6 V batteries; replaceable battery 6x 3.6 V (op 2.9–3.6 V)\n  \n  I'll model with config_level: axis \"transmitter type / power supply option\". Entries:\n  - attribute: power_supply_voltage_ac, axis: \"power_supply_option\", condition: \"AC mains\", value {min:95, max:240}, unit \"V\"\n  - attribute: power_supply_frequency, condition \"AC mains\", {min:50,max:60}, \"Hz\"\n  - attribute: power_supply_voltage_dc, condition \"renewable energy power (DC powered)\", {min:6,max:32}, \"V\"\n  - attribute: battery_count... \"2x 3,6 V\" — battery quantity 2 or 6, battery voltage 3.6 V, operating range 2.9–3.6 V.\n    - attribute: replaceable_battery_quantity: FEW41/43: 2; FEW45: 6 — varies by transmitter → could be config_level with axis transmitter_type.\n    - attribute: battery_voltage: 3.6 V\n    - attribute: battery_operating_voltage_range: {min: 2.9, max: 3.6} V\n- checking_facilities: \"Type P (permanent) automatic checking facility\"\n- software_identification: \"see tables 3/4\" — omit.\n\nThe X/Y/Z variants: X=1 standard, 3 advanced, 5 premium (mobile comms MC); Y ∈ {1,2,8,9} integral/remote/sensor only; Z ∈ {R, V, F} reduced/virtual full/full bore. This describes model family.\n\nModel family: The family here is defined by transmitter type FEW41Y.Z / FEW43Y.Z / FEW45Y.Z, bore R/V/F, and meter size DN40–DN1800, power configuration. This is huge. The matrix tables (Tables 5–8) give flow rates per DN size × accuracy class × power config × bore.\n\nHow to structure models? The model matrix is complex: size (DN40..DN1800) × bore (reduced/full) × power (AC/battery) × accuracy class (1/2). The natural approach: matrix_tables preserve Tables 5–12 as-is, and model_family models could be the DN sizes? But models differ also by bore/power.\n\nGiven the guidance, I think the cleanest: model_family.family_name = \"AquaMaster4\", models[] entries for the transmitter variants: FEW41Y.Z, FEW43Y.Z, FEW45Y.Z? Actually the type designation is AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z and FET4*. The tables distinguish three transmitter groups: FEW41Y.Z, FEW43Y.Z (Table 1/3), FEW45Y.Z (Table 2/4). But the flow tables 5–8 are organized by bore × power.\n\nFor model_level characteristics, the main matrix is the flow-rate tables: q1, q2, q3, q4, ratio_q3_q1 per (size, accuracy class, power config, bore). That's 4-dimensional. Rather than explode model_level for hundreds of combos, I should preserve them in matrix_tables (Tables 5–8) and add a modest model_level maybe not.\n\nThe schema says matrix_tables: \"preserve multi-dim tables that don't fit elsewhere\". Tables 5–8 are 2-D tables (size × flow rates) but with the caveat that each is per (bore, power) combination and rows have per-class variants. I'll create 4 matrix_tables:\n\n1. \"Flow characteristics — reduced bore, AC mains powered\" (Table 5)\n2. \"Flow characteristics — reduced bore, battery / renewable energy powered\" (Table 6)\n3. \"Flow characteristics — full bore, AC mains powered\" (Table 7)\n4. \"Flow characteristics — full bore, battery / renewable energy powered\" (Table 8)\n\nEach with columns: meter_size, accuracy_class, q1_minimum, q2_transitional, q3_permanent, q4_overload, ratio_q3_q1. Units: m³/h for flow rates; ratio dimensionless.\n\nRows: each (size, class) row. For \"1 & 2\" accuracy class rows, I'll set accuracy_class \"1 & 2\" as string. Values numeric with decimal conversion (0,05 → 0.05).\n\nAlso Tables 9–12 (indicating device): columns meter_size(s), indicating_range_max, indicating_range_min, verification_scale_interval_max. Units: mm for size, m³ for others. 4 more matrix_tables.\n\nThat's 8 matrix tables total. Plus software tables 3 & 4? Those are software identification tables — could be matrix_tables too:\n- \"Software identification — AquaMaster4 FEW41Y.Z and FEW43Y.Z\" with columns software_part_code, software_version, crc_checksum, remarks. Rows grouped by part code. Software part codes: 3KXF208402U0113 (Full functionality) versions 03.00.03, 03.00.04, 03.02.00, 03.03.02, 03.03.03, 03.03.06, 03.04.00, 03.04.02; 3KXF208402U0313 (Sensus / Logger / Pressure / VKL sensor updater) 02.00.02, 02.00.04; 3KXF208402U0513 (Modbus / Pressure / VKL sensor updater) 02.00.01, 02.00.05.\n- \"Software identification — AquaMaster4 FEW45Y.Z\" with MCU category (Measurement/4G/NB-IoT), version, checksum, remarks. Footnote [1] applies.\n\nI'll include these as matrix_tables too. That gives 10 matrix tables. That's a lot of JSON but it's faithful extraction. The instruction says preserve multi-dim tables that don't fit elsewhere — yes, include them.\n\nShould I also populate model_family.models? The models: given type designations, the family covers transmitter types FEW41Y.Z, FEW43Y.Z, FEW45Y.Z with Y ∈ {1,2,8,9}, Z ∈ {R,V,F}. Enumerating all combos would be X(1,3,5) × Y(1,2,8,9) × Z(R,V,F) = 36 transmitter variants plus sensor FET4*. That's too many and speculative.\n\nI think model_family: family_name \"AquaMaster4\", models: list the distinct named types: \n- {model_id: \"AquaMaster4 FEW41Y.Z\", attributes: {variant: \"standard\"}}? Hmm.\n\nActually the cert explicitly names three transmitter types in tables: FEW41Y.Z, FEW43Y.Z, FEW45Y.Z. And type designation pattern FEW4XY.Z / FET4*. For models, I could list:\n- FEW41Y.Z (standard transmitter)\n- FEW43Y.Z \n- FEW45Y.Z (premium mobile comms)\n\nWait — X: 1=standard, 3=advanced, 5=premium (mobile Comms MC). So FEW41Y.Z = standard, FEW43Y.Z = advanced, FEW45Y.Z = premium MC.\n\nmodels:\n- {model_id: \"FEW41Y.Z\", label: \"AquaMaster4 standard transmitter\", attributes: {x_variant: \"1 (standard)\"}}\n- {model_id: \"FEW43Y.Z\", label: \"AquaMaster4 advanced transmitter\", attributes: {x_variant: \"3 (advanced)\"}}\n- {model_id: \"FEW45Y.Z\", label: \"AquaMaster4 premium transmitter (mobile comms)\", attributes: {x_variant: \"5 (premium, mobile comms - MC)\"}}\n\nAnd maybe sensor \"FET4*\". Also DN sizes are \"meter size\" not models per se. I'll keep models as transmitter variants.\n\nConfig-level characteristics: For power supply and EMC environment differences between FEW41/43 and FEW45, I'll use config_level with axis \"transmitter_type\".\n\nLet me now write the components: The measuring instrument consists of flow sensor (FET4) and flow transmitter/indicating device (FEW4). The cert names: \"flow sensor and flow transmitter\" in temperature; \"indicating device\" tables. Components:\n- role: \"measuring sensor / flow sensor\", type_designations: [\"FET4\"]\n- role: \"transmitter / calculating and indicating device\", type_designations: [\"FEW41Y.Z\", \"FEW43Y.Z\", \"FEW45Y.Z\"], alternatives: \"OR\"\n- role: \"indicating device\" — hmm, the transmitter includes the indicating device. Tables 9-12 describe \"the indicating device\". I could add component role \"indicating device\" with no separate designation. Maybe skip; or include as part of transmitter. I'll include two components: flow sensor and flow transmitter (calculating and indicating device). Also revision 9 mentions \"AquaMaster4 FEW45Y.Z Mobile comms variant of calculating and indicating device\". Good support.\n\nCharacteristics — type_level:\n\nLet me define:\n- measuring_principle: \"Electromagnetic\" (string value)\n- accuracy_class: \"1 and 2\" — or skip; recommendation already has accuracy_classes [\"1\",\"2\"]. I'll skip in type_level to avoid duplication? The rules say don't fabricate but duplication isn't fabrication. I'll include accuracy_class at type_level as string \"1 and 2\". Hmm, structured value requires object shape: {value: \"1 and 2\", unit_symbol: null}. Fine.\n- environmental_class: {value: \"O (installed outdoors)\"}\n- electromagnetic_environment: {value: \"E2\"} plus config for FEW45.\n- mechanical_class: {value: \"M1\"}\n- ambient_temperature_range: {value: {min:-25, max:55}, unit_symbol: \"°C\"}\n- water_temperature_class: {value: \"T50\"}; water_temperature_range: {value: {min:0.1, max:50}, unit_symbol:\"°C\"}\n- maximum_admissible_pressure: {value: 1.6, unit_symbol: \"MPa\"}; maximum_admissible_pressure_alt_bar? I'll just do MAP in MPa and mention (16 bar)? Keep one entry: value 1.6, unit \"MPa\". Maybe add secondary: \"maximum_admissible_pressure_bar\": 16 bar. I'll skip the alt to reduce noise. Actually the parenthetical is meaningful. I'll keep just MPa.\n- orientation: {value: \"All positions (horizontal, vertical, or diagonal)\"}\n- reverse_flow: {value: \"intended to measure reverse flow\"}\n- checking_facility: {value: \"Type P (permanent) automatic checking facility\"}\n\nConfig_level:\n1. electromagnetic_environment, axis \"transmitter type / antenna cable\": \n   - {condition: \"FEW45Y.Z with external antenna cable\", value: \"E1\"}\n   - {condition: \"FEW45Y.Z without external antenna cable\", value: \"E2\"}\n   - {condition: \"FEW41Y.Z / FEW43Y.Z\", value: \"E2\"}\n2. flow_profile_sensitivity_class, axis \"bore / size\":\n   - {condition: \"default (0 x DN upstream, 0 x DN downstream)\", value: \"U0 and D0\"}\n   - {condition: \"full bore DN250 and larger\", value: \"U3 and D0 (3 x DN upstream, 0 x DN downstream)\"}\n3. pressure_loss_class, axis \"bore / meter size\":\n   - {condition: \"reduced bore, DN40–DN50\", value: \"Δp 40 (0.40 bar)\"}\n   - {condition: \"reduced bore, larger than DN50\", value: \"Δp 63 (0.63 bar)\"}\n   - {condition: \"full bore, DN40–DN50\", value: \"Δp 10 (0.10 bar)\"}\n   - {condition: \"full bore, DN65–DN200\", value: \"Δp 16 (0.16 bar)\"}\n   - {condition: \"full bore, DN250 and larger\", value: \"Δp 10 (0.10 bar)\"}\n   \n   Should value be structured? The schema for config_level values: `{condition, value}` — example earlier says \"Values follow the same structured form: {condition, value, unit_symbol}\". So each value should be {value:..., unit_symbol:...}? The schema line: `config_level[]: attribute, axis, values[]: {condition, value}`. And rule 5: values follow the same structured form. For class strings, value is a string. For pressure loss with bar, I can do value: \"Δp 40\", plus... it's messy. I'll keep value as string \"Δp 40 (0.40 bar)\". Or separate numeric: value: 0.40, unit bar, with class in condition? Condition: \"reduced bore, DN40 and DN50, pressure loss class Δp 40\", value 0.40 bar. Hmm. I'll do value string including bar, it's a class designation primarily. Actually let me do: {\"condition\": \"reduced bore, DN40 and DN50\", \"value\": \"Δp 40\", \"unit_symbol\": null, \"detail\": \"0.40 bar\"}? No — stick to schema: {condition, value, unit_symbol, footnote_markers}. I'll put value as string \"Δp 40 (0.40 bar)\".\n\n4. Power supply — config_level entries with axis \"power supply option\" and transmitter type:\n   For simplicity, since AC and DC values are identical across transmitter types (95–240 VAC, 50/60 Hz, 6–32 V DC), and only battery count differs (2x vs 6x):\n   - attribute: ac_mains_voltage, axis: \"power supply option\", values: [{condition: \"AC mains\", value: {min:95, max:240}, unit_symbol: \"V\"}]\n   - attribute: ac_mains_frequency, ...: [{condition: \"AC mains\", value: {min:50, max:60}, unit_symbol: \"Hz\"}]\n   - attribute: dc_supply_voltage, ...: [{condition: \"renewable energy power (DC powered)\", value: {min:6, max:32}, unit_symbol: \"V\"}]\n   - attribute: replaceable_battery_configuration, axis \"transmitter type\": [{condition: \"FEW41Y.Z / FEW43Y.Z\", value: \"2x 3.6 V (operating range 2.9–3.6 V)\"}, {condition: \"FEW45Y.Z\", value: \"6x 3.6 V (operating range 2.9–3.6 V)\"}]\n   - Also FEW45 with AC/DC includes 4x 3.6 V replaceable batteries — backup. Maybe add condition entries: battery_backup... I'll incorporate into the battery config strings? That complicates. I'll add a separate attribute:\n     - attribute: replaceable_battery_backup (integral with AC/DC powered), axis transmitter type: FEW45: \"4x 3.6 V\". Only FEW45 has this.\n   \n   Let me structure power supply config entries:\n   - {attribute: \"power_supply_voltage\", axis: \"power_supply_option\", values: [{condition: \"AC mains\", value: {min:95,max:240}, unit_symbol:\"V\"}, {condition: \"renewable energy (DC powered)\", value: {min:6,max:32}, unit_symbol:\"V\"}]}\n   - {attribute: \"power_supply_frequency\", axis: \"power_supply_option\", values: [{condition: \"AC mains\", value: {min:50,max:60}, unit_symbol:\"Hz\"}]}\n   - {attribute: \"replaceable_battery\", axis: \"transmitter_type\", values: [{condition: \"FEW41Y.Z / FEW43Y.Z\", value: \"2x 3.6 V (operating range: 2.9–3.6 V)\"}, {condition: \"FEW45Y.Z\", value: \"6x 3.6 V (operating range: 2.9–3.6 V)\"}]}\n   - {attribute: \"battery_backup_with_external_power\", axis: \"transmitter_type\", values: [{condition: \"FEW45Y.Z AC mains or renewable energy powered\", value: \"4x 3.6 V replaceable batteries\"}]}\n\nHmm, but \"power supply voltage\" 95-240 VAC — unit V. Fine.\n\nModel_level: Should I emit model_level for the flow rates? That would be enormous (Tables 5–8 combined have ~50+26+33+27 ≈ 135 size-class rows × 5 attributes). Not feasible/appropriate. The matrix_tables capture it. The R137 example shows both matrix_tables AND model_level, but that was a small table (2 models). Here it's a huge family; I'll rely on matrix_tables and define models as transmitter variants with a few model_level attributes where they differ:\n\nModel-level differences between FEW41Y.Z/FEW43Y.Z and FEW45Y.Z:\n- EMC environment (handled in config)\n- battery count (config)\n- software tables differ (matrix tables)\n- power supply (FEW45 batteries included)\n\nI could do model_level for:\n- electromagnetic_environment: FEW41Y.Z: E2, FEW43Y.Z: E2, FEW45Y.Z: \"E1 (with external antenna cable) / E2 (without)\". Meh.\n\nI think config_level handles it. I'll skip model_level or add minimal one. Let me add model_level for transmitter-related attributes that cleanly vary:\n\n- attribute \"software_identification_table\": FEW41Y.Z→\"Table 3\", FEW43Y.Z→\"Table 3\", FEW45Y.Z→\"Table 4\". That's meta. Skip.\n\nI'll omit model_level[] since matrix_tables carry the size matrices and config_level carries transmitter differences. Actually wait — rule 4 says detect matrix and put varying attributes in model_level not type_level. The flow rates vary per size — these are \"models\" in a sense (DN sizes). Emitting model_level for q1/q2/q3/q4 per size would be ~30 sizes × 2 classes × 4 configs... way too much. The matrix_tables approach with a note is the pragmatic choice. I'll include a few model_level entries? No — consistency: I'll define models in model_family by meter size? The models list would be huge too.\n\nDecision: model_family with models = transmitter variants (FEW41Y.Z, FEW43Y.Z, FEW45Y.Z) + note attributes. matrix_tables hold all 12 tables (flow ×4, indicating device ×4, software ×2... actually software tables 3 and 4 → 2 tables). Total matrix_tables: 4 flow + 4 indicating + 2 software = 10.\n\nHmm, software tables: Table 3 spans pages 4-5 (continuation). I'll merge into one matrix_table. Table 4 on page 5.\n\nLet me now carefully transcribe the tables.\n\nTable 5 (reduced bore, AC powered) rows (size, class, q1, q2, q3, q4, ratio):\n- DN40, 1: 0.05, 0.08, 25, 31.25, 500\n- DN40, 2: 0.025, 0.04, 25, 31.25, 1000\n- DN50, 1: 0.08, 0.128, 40, 50, 500\n- DN50, 2: 0.04, 0.064, 40, 50, 1000\n- DN65, 1 & 2: 0.063, 0.1008, 63, 78.75, 1000\n- DN80, 1 & 2: 0.1, 0.16, 100, 125, 1000\n- DN100, 1: 0.32, 0.512, 160, 200, 500\n- DN100, 2: 0.16, 0.256, 160, 200, 1000\n- DN125, 1: 0.32, 0.512, 160, 200, 500\n- DN125, 2: 0.16, 0.256, 160, 200, 1000\n- DN150, 1 & 2: 0.4, 0.64, 400, 500, 1000\n- DN200, 1 & 2: 0.63, 1.008, 630, 787.5, 1000\n- DN250, 1: 2, 3.2, 1000, 1250, 500\n- DN250, 2: 1, 1.6, 1000, 1250, 1000\n- DN300, 1: 3.2, 5.12, 1600, 2000, 500\n- DN300, 2: 1.6, 2.56, 1600, 2000, 1000\n- DN350, 1: 3.2, 5.12, 1600, 2000, 500\n- DN350, 2: 1.6, 2.56, 1600, 2000, 1000\n- DN400, 1 & 2: 5, 8, 2500, 3125, 500\n- DN4